            "In accomplice studies such as Word et al. (1974), both participants and accomplices should be treated as random factors, but the interviewers (accomplices) were not treated as random, so the results can only be generalized to the specific accomplices included in the study.",
            "With such small numbers of accomplices as in Word et al. (1974) (three White and two African American interviewers), it probably would not have been feasible to treat them as random, and the numbers of levels of the random factors jointly constrain power in mixed-model analyses (Westfall et al., 2014).",
            "In Stephenson and Wicklund's (1984) study of the contagion of self-focus, treating the non-instructed dyad member as random also treats the other member of the dyad as random, because the unit in the analysis is effectively the yoked pair of participants.",
